 "literal": "Good beauty to be lost, beauty greatly having wasted away, / sweet life even if it passes, do not say [anything against him], he to us / mother and father and are they not, O friend? / Sulking, what is that, when there is no love?",
 "translation": "Even if my fine beauty is lost and I waste away greatly, and even if my sweet life departs, do not speak ill of him. O friend, is he not like a mother and a father to us? What is the use of sulking when there is no love?",
 "modern": "தோழி! என் நல்ல அழகு அழிந்து, நான் மிகவும் மெலிந்து, என் இனிய உயிர் பிரிந்தாலும் அவரைப் பற்றித் தவறாகப் பேசாதே. அவர் நமக்குத் தாயும் தந்தையும் போன்றவர் அல்லவா? அவரிடம் அன்பு இல்லாதபோது, நாம் ஊடல் கொள்வதால் என்ன பயன்?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante in a Marutam (agricultural) landscape setting. The confidante has criticized the hero for his indifference, which has caused the heroine to waste away. The heroine defends him, saying that even if her beauty is completely lost and she dies, she cannot bear to hear him spoken of unkindly. She compares him to her mother and father, who are to be respected and loved unconditionally, regardless of their actions. She concludes that sulking (pulavi) is only meaningful when there is mutual love; if he has no love for her, there is no point in showing anger or resentment. Key words include 'annai' (mother), 'attan' (father), and 'pulavi' (sulking).",
